// Sample full-mark answers, keyed by question id (plus part label for a
// multi-part question). Kept in their own file so that re-extracting a past
// paper never overwrites them. Key words a marker looks for are in <b>bold</b>.

window.MODEL_ANSWERS = window.MODEL_ANSWERS || {};
Object.assign(window.MODEL_ANSWERS, {
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun18-02|02.5": "The x axis is labelled <b>Time in minutes</b> with an <b>even linear scale</b> added, and all of person S's points are <b>plotted correctly</b> to within <b>half a small square</b>. The points are then joined <b>point to point with a ruler</b>, or with a <b>smooth curved line of best fit</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun18-05|05.2": "Without magnesium the plant cannot make enough <b>chlorophyll</b>, so the leaves look <b>yellow</b>. With less chlorophyll <b>less light is absorbed</b>, so the <b>rate of photosynthesis</b> falls and the plant makes <b>less glucose</b>. Because less glucose is available to be converted into <b>proteins</b>, there is less material for new cells and the plant's <b>growth is stunted</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun18-05|05.3": "The scientist injects the PVY <b>protein</b> (the antigen) into a <b>mouse</b>, which makes <b>lymphocytes</b> that produce the antibody against it. These lymphocytes are then fused with <b>tumour cells</b> to form <b>hybridoma cells</b>. The hybridoma making the <b>specific antibody</b> to PVY is selected and <b>cloned</b> so that large amounts of the monoclonal antibody can be collected.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-02|02.6": "The meat-free burger is better in some ways because it contains more <b>fibre</b>, which <b>aids digestion</b> and prevents constipation, and it contains less <b>fat</b> and less <b>cholesterol</b>, which cause <b>narrowing of the arteries</b> and so raise the risk of <b>coronary heart disease</b> and of <b>obesity</b>, which is itself a risk factor for <b>type 2 diabetes</b>. However, the meat burger supplies more <b>protein</b>, which is needed for <b>growth and repair</b>, and both burgers give similar <b>carbohydrate</b> for energy, and neither set of data shows the <b>vitamins and minerals</b> present. Overall, the meat-free burger fits a healthy balanced diet better, provided enough protein is eaten from other foods.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-03|03.3": "<b>magnification = size of image ÷ size of real object</b>\nso size of real object = size of image ÷ magnification\nsize of real object = 4.8 ÷ 400\nsize of real object = 0.012 cm\n0.012 cm = 0.012 × 10 000 µm\nreal length of cell Z = <b>120 µm</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-04|04.4": "Before the cell divides, the <b>DNA is replicated</b> so that there are two copies of every <b>chromosome</b>. The <b>copied chromosomes are pulled apart</b> to opposite ends of the cell and two <b>nuclei</b> form. Finally the <b>cytoplasm and cell membrane divide</b>, giving two cells that each contain an <b>identical set of chromosomes</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-05|05.4": "At midnight the stomata are almost <b>closed</b> because there is <b>no light for photosynthesis</b>, so carbon dioxide is not needed and closing them <b>reduces water loss</b> by transpiration. At midday the stomata are <b>open widest</b> because the <b>light intensity is at its maximum</b>, so the plant can take in <b>more carbon dioxide</b> for a faster <b>rate of photosynthesis</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-06|06.4": "Organism D is smaller, so it has a <b>larger surface area to volume ratio</b> and therefore <b>loses heat more quickly</b> per unit volume than organism E. To stay at a constant body temperature D must <b>release more heat</b>, so it needs a <b>higher rate of respiration</b>, and because <b>respiration is a large part of metabolism</b> its metabolic rate is greater.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-07|07.6": "A vaccine containing <b>inactive</b> or dead HIV, or a part of the virus, is injected into the body. The <b>white blood cells</b> (lymphocytes) respond by making <b>antibodies</b> against its <b>antigens</b>, and <b>memory cells</b> remain. If the person later meets the live virus, the correct <b>specific antibodies</b> are produced <b>quickly and in large numbers</b> and destroy the virus before it can cause infection.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun22-07|07.7": "The HIV <b>antigen</b> or protein is injected into a <b>mouse</b>, and the mouse's <b>lymphocytes</b> that make the <b>specific antibody</b> to HIV are collected. These lymphocytes are combined with a <b>tumour cell</b> to create a <b>hybridoma</b>, which can both make the antibody and divide. The hybridoma is then <b>cloned</b> to produce many cells, and the monoclonal antibody they make is collected and purified.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-01|01.5": "mass of chemical A in 100 g of normal leaves = 1.2 g\nmass in leaves with chlorosis = 0.6 × 1.2 = <b>0.72 g per 100 g</b>\nmass in 200 g = 0.72 × 2 = 1.44 g\nconverting to milligrams: 1.44 × 1000\nmass of chemical A = <b>1440 mg</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-02|02.8": "A partly blocked <b>coronary artery</b> carries <b>less blood</b> to the heart muscle, so the muscle cells receive <b>less oxygen and glucose</b>. This means <b>less aerobic respiration</b> takes place, so <b>less energy is transferred</b> and the heart muscle <b>contracts less strongly</b>. The heart therefore pumps less blood around the body, so body cells also get less oxygen and glucose and <b>carbon dioxide is removed more slowly</b>, causing <b>tiredness and breathlessness</b>. The heart muscle may respire <b>anaerobically</b>, producing <b>lactic acid</b>, which builds up and causes chest pain.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-03|03.2": "When an animal is vaccinated, its <b>white blood cells</b> produce <b>antibodies</b> that are <b>specific</b> to the <b>antigens</b> on Salmonella, and <b>memory cells</b> remain. If the animal later meets live Salmonella, these antibodies are produced <b>quickly and in large numbers</b> and the bacteria are destroyed, so <b>fewer bacteria are present</b> in the animals and in their meat, milk or eggs. People therefore eat far fewer Salmonella, so the number never becomes high enough to cause infection or produce <b>toxins</b>, and no outbreak occurs.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-04|04.1": "Anaerobic respiration causes a build-up of <b>lactic acid</b> in the muscles, which leads to <b>muscle fatigue</b> so the muscles stop contracting efficiently. It also creates an <b>oxygen debt</b>, so the person continues to <b>breathe deeply and quickly</b> afterwards to supply the <b>oxygen needed to break down the lactic acid</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-04|04.2": "I would take a <b>large group of athletes</b> of similar age, sex and fitness with no health problems, and get <b>each athlete to do all</b> of at least <b>two different types of exercise</b>, such as running and cycling. I would <b>record the heart rate before and after</b> each exercise and work out the <b>increase in heart rate</b> for every athlete. I would then calculate the <b>mean increase</b> for each type of exercise and <b>compare the means</b>. To make it a <b>fair test</b> I would keep the <b>time spent exercising</b> the same and allow the same rest and caffeine intake between tests.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun23-06|06.9": "<b>Chlorophyll absorbs most</b> of the blue light, about <b>80%</b> according to Figure 10, so the discs in blue light <b>photosynthesised fastest</b> and took the <b>least time to rise</b> to the surface. Chlorophyll <b>absorbs very little green light</b> and reflects most of it, so those discs photosynthesised <b>slowest</b> and took the <b>longest time to rise</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-01|01.7": "The y axis is labelled <b>Percentage increase in risk compared with people who have never smoked</b> and given a <b>linear scale of 1 cm = 5%</b> that fits all the data. Every bar is then <b>plotted to within half a small square</b> of its value from Table 1 and each bar is <b>labelled with its cardiovascular disease</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-02|02.3": "Because less <b>lipase</b> reaches the small intestine, less <b>fat</b> is broken down into <b>fatty acids and glycerol</b>; less <b>amylase</b> means less <b>starch</b> is broken down into <b>glucose</b>; and less <b>protease</b> means less <b>protein</b> is broken down into <b>amino acids</b>. These large molecules are <b>insoluble</b>, so far fewer small soluble molecules are <b>absorbed</b> into the blood. The person therefore has fewer <b>amino acids for building new proteins</b> and muscle, stores less fat and carries out <b>less respiration</b>, so has less energy for growth and finds it hard to gain body mass.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-03|03.1": "First I would grind the cake with water and filter it to make a solution. To test for <b>starch</b> I would add a few drops of <b>iodine solution</b> to a sample; it turns from <b>orange-brown to blue-black</b> if starch is present. To test for <b>sugar</b> I would add <b>Benedict's solution</b> to a second sample and <b>heat it in a water bath at about 80 °C</b>; it changes from <b>blue to brick red</b> if sugar is present. For safety I would use a <b>water bath rather than a naked flame</b> to avoid burns and scalds, <b>wear eye protection</b> because Benedict's solution is an <b>irritant</b> and can spit when heated, point the tube away from people, and wipe up spills at once.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-05|05.1": "I would cut potato pieces of the <b>same size and mass</b> from the <b>same potato</b> with the skin removed, and <b>measure the mass</b> of each one with a balance. I would put one piece into each of at least <b>five different concentrations of salt solution</b> and leave them all for the <b>same length of time</b>. I would then remove each piece, <b>blot it dry</b> in the same way, reweigh it and work out the <b>change in mass</b>, or better the <b>percentage change in mass</b>. I would <b>repeat each concentration three times</b> and calculate a <b>mean</b> before plotting the results.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-07|07.1": "Leaf 1 was covered with black paper so it received <b>no light</b> and <b>could not photosynthesise</b>. Leaf 2 had no <b>carbon dioxide</b> available, so it also could not photosynthesise. In both leaves <b>no glucose was made</b>, so none could be <b>converted into starch</b> and the starch test was negative. Leaf 3 had both <b>light and carbon dioxide</b>, so it photosynthesised, made glucose and stored it as <b>starch</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-07|07.8": "Raising the temperature while the carbon dioxide concentration stays constant <b>increases the rate of photosynthesis</b>, because the particles gain <b>kinetic energy</b> and collide more often, so <b>enzyme activity</b> increases. Increasing the carbon dioxide concentration also increases the rate because there is more <b>reactant</b> available. Each curve eventually <b>levels off</b> because another factor, such as light intensity, becomes <b>limiting</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun24-08|08.3": "length = volume ÷ (π × radius<sup>2</sup>)\nlength = 24 500 000 ÷ (3.14 × 125<sup>2</sup>)\nlength = 24 500 000 ÷ 49 062.5 = <b>499.4 nm</b>\n<b>magnification = size of image ÷ real size</b>\nsize of image = 4 mm = 4 000 000 nm\nmagnification = 4 000 000 ÷ 499.4\nmagnification = <b>×8010</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-02|02.5": "At 30 °C the iodine solution stayed yellow-brown because <b>no starch was present</b>, since this is close to the <b>optimum temperature</b> and the <b>amylase broke all the starch down</b> into sugars. At 90 °C the iodine turned <b>blue-black</b> because <b>starch was still present</b>; the high temperature had <b>denatured the amylase</b>, changing the shape of its <b>active site</b> so it could no longer break starch down.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-04|04.2": "rate = number of bubbles ÷ time\nrate = 10 ÷ 43\nrate = 0.23255... bubbles per second\nto 3 significant figures, rate = <b>0.233 bubbles per second</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-05|05.2": "<b>magnification = size of image ÷ size of real object</b>\nso size of real object = size of image ÷ magnification\nsize of real object = 6.3 ÷ 7200\nsize of real object = 0.000875 cm\n0.000875 cm = 0.000875 × 10 000 µm\nreal diameter = <b>8.75 µm</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-06|06.2": "The many gill filaments give a very <b>large surface area</b> for diffusion. They have a rich <b>capillary network</b>, and the flowing blood <b>maintains a steep concentration gradient</b> for oxygen. The filaments and capillaries have <b>thin walls</b>, and the water flows <b>very close to the blood</b>, so there is a <b>short diffusion distance</b> and oxygen is absorbed quickly.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-06|06.3": "When the fish swims there is much more <b>movement</b>, so its muscles must carry out more <b>muscle contraction</b>. Contraction needs <b>more energy</b>, which has to be <b>transferred by respiration</b>, so the <b>rate of respiration increases</b>. Respiration is a major part of <b>metabolism</b>, so the fish's metabolic rate is higher than when it is resting.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-07|07.8": "A damaged liver produces <b>less bile</b>. Bile normally <b>emulsifies</b> fat, breaking it into <b>small droplets</b> that give a much <b>larger surface area</b> for <b>lipase</b> to work on, so with less bile the lipase <b>digests fat much more slowly</b>. Bile is also <b>alkaline</b> and <b>neutralises the acid</b> from the stomach, so without it the small intestine stays acidic and lipase is no longer at its <b>optimum pH</b>. The enzyme may even be <b>denatured</b>, so its active site changes shape and fat digestion is slower still.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-jun25-08|08.3": "All three antibiotics have <b>little effect for the first four or five hours</b>. <b>Antibiotic C is the most effective</b> because it starts to reduce the number of bacteria <b>soonest and fastest</b> and is the only one to reduce the bacteria to <b>zero</b>. <b>Antibiotic B is more effective than A</b> but less effective than C, as it begins to work after C but before A. <b>Antibiotic A is the least effective</b>, reaching the <b>highest number of bacteria</b> and still leaving many alive at 16 hours.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-nov20-03|03.6": "In the first stage the cell grows, the number of <b>sub-cellular structures</b> such as <b>mitochondria and ribosomes</b> increases, and the <b>chromosomes are copied</b> so there are two of each. In the second stage, <b>mitosis</b>, one copy of each chromosome is <b>pulled to each end of the cell</b>. In the final stage the <b>cytoplasm and cell membranes divide</b> to form two identical daughter cells.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-nov20-05|05.4": "One reason is a lack of <b>magnesium ions</b>, so the plant cannot make enough <b>chlorophyll</b>; the leaves turn yellow and there is <b>less photosynthesis</b>, so not enough <b>glucose</b> is made to build the proteins needed for growth. Another reason is infestation by <b>aphids</b>, which pierce the stem and <b>remove sugars from the phloem</b>, so again there is <b>not enough glucose</b> to make proteins or to release energy for growth, and the plant stays small.",
  "aqa-8461-1h-nov21-02|02.3": "radius of clear zone measured from the plate = <b>1.1 cm</b>\n<b>area = π × radius<sup>2</sup></b>\narea = 3.14 × 1.1<sup>2</sup>\narea = 3.14 × 1.21\narea = <b>3.80 cm<sup>2</sup></b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-nov21-06|06.7": "reading the tangent drawn at 12:00, volume rises by 40 cm<sup>3</sup> in 7 hours\n7 hours = 7 × 60 = 420 minutes\nrate = 40 ÷ 420\nrate = 0.0952 cm<sup>3</sup> per minute\nin standard form, rate = <b>9.52 × 10<sup>−2</sup> cm<sup>3</sup> per minute</b>",
  "aqa-8461-1h-nov21-07|07.7": "The blocked tube means <b>less bile reaches the small intestine</b>. Bile normally <b>emulsifies</b> fat into <b>small droplets</b>, giving a <b>larger surface area</b> for <b>lipase</b> to act on, so without it fat is broken down much more slowly. Bile is also <b>alkaline</b> and <b>neutralises the stomach acid</b>, so the small intestine is no longer at the <b>optimum pH</b> for lipase and the enzyme works far less effectively.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-02|02.1": "Two <b>abiotic</b> factors that could affect the earthworm population are the <b>amount of water</b> (moisture) in the soil and the <b>temperature</b> of the soil. Two <b>biotic</b> factors are the <b>amount of dead and decaying plant matter</b> available as food and the number of <b>predators</b> such as birds.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-02|02.2": "I would place a <b>quadrat</b> of the <b>same area</b>, for example 0.25 m<sup>2</sup>, at a position in area A chosen <b>randomly</b> using <b>random number coordinates</b>. I would pour the <b>same volume</b> of the <b>same concentration</b> of chemical X onto the soil inside the quadrat, wait the <b>same length of time</b>, then <b>collect and count</b> all the worms that come to the surface. I would take at <b>least five samples</b> in each area and repeat the whole method in area B. Finally I would calculate the <b>mean number of worms per m<sup>2</sup></b> for each area and <b>compare the means</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-04|04.3": "<b>Carbon dioxide and methane</b> released into the air are <b>greenhouse gases</b> that trap heat and cause <b>global warming</b>, leading to melting ice caps, rising sea levels and <b>loss of habitat</b>. <b>Acidic gases</b> such as sulfur dioxide cause <b>acid rain</b>, which damages leaves so there is less photosynthesis, and they also damage people's <b>lungs</b>, causing breathing difficulties and asthma. <b>Particulates</b> block light from leaves. In water, <b>sewage</b> lets bacteria multiply and <b>use up the oxygen</b>, while <b>fertiliser</b> causes <b>algae</b> to grow and block the light, so plants die and fish suffocate.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-05|05.2": "Persons 7 and 8 are both <b>carriers</b> with the genotype <b>Nn</b>, so each produces gametes carrying <b>N</b> and <b>n</b>. Putting these on the two sides of a <b>Punnett square</b> gives offspring genotypes <b>NN, Nn, Nn and nn</b>. NN and Nn do <b>not have MSUD</b>, while only <b>nn has MSUD</b>, so the probability that the fourth child has MSUD is <b>0.25</b>, or 25%.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-06|06.4": "efficiency of grass = (energy used for growth ÷ energy input) × 100\nefficiency of grass = (21 800 ÷ 1 700 000) × 100\nefficiency of grass = <b>1.2824%</b>\nnumber of times more efficient = 4.098 ÷ 1.2824\nnumber of times more efficient = 3.196...\nto 3 significant figures the cow is <b>3.20</b> times more efficient",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun22-07|07.3": "volume of the counting grid square = 0.2 × 0.2 × 0.1\nvolume = <b>0.004 mm<sup>3</sup></b>\ncells in 1 mm<sup>3</sup> of diluted water = 14 ÷ 0.004\ncells in 1 mm<sup>3</sup> of diluted water = 3500\nthe pond water was diluted by a factor of 4, so multiply by 4\ncells in 1 mm<sup>3</sup> of undiluted pond water = 3500 × 4 = <b>14 000</b>",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-02|02.6": "I would use the <b>ruler drop test</b>, holding a ruler so that zero is level with the top of the person's thumb and recording the distance it falls before they catch it. I would test each student <b>without coffee</b> and again after drinking coffee, <b>repeat at least three times</b> each and test <b>several students</b>, then calculate and <b>compare the mean</b> distances. To make it <b>valid</b> I would keep the <b>volume and strength of coffee</b>, the <b>time between drinking and testing</b> and the hand used the same for everyone.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-03|03.9": "The farmer chooses the <b>best birds from variety A and variety B</b>, those laying the most eggs and growing fastest for meat, and <b>breeds them together</b>. From their offspring he <b>selects the ones with the highest egg numbers and the heaviest bodies</b> and breeds those together. He then <b>repeats this over many generations</b> until the new variety reliably shows both characteristics.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-04|04.4": "drawing a <b>tangent</b> to the 15 °C curve at day 2\nchange in pH read from the tangent = 6.5 − 4.5 = 2.0\ntime taken = 4 days\nrate at 15 °C = 2.0 ÷ 4 = 0.5 pH units per day\nnumber of times faster = 0.5 ÷ 0.3\nthe rate at 15 °C is <b>1.67</b> times faster",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-05|05.3": "With less <b>ADH</b> the <b>kidney tubules become less permeable to water</b>, so <b>less water is reabsorbed</b> back into the blood. More water therefore stays in the urine, which <b>increases the volume and rate of urine production</b>. Because this extra water dilutes the urine, the <b>concentration of dissolved substances</b> in it falls.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-06|06.1": "From 0 to 6 hours it is <b>dark</b>, so <b>only respiration</b> takes place in the plants and this <b>releases carbon dioxide</b>, making the concentration in the air rise. From 8 to 12 hours it is <b>light</b>, so the <b>rate of photosynthesis is faster than the rate of respiration</b>, giving a <b>net uptake of carbon dioxide</b>, so the concentration falls.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun23-07|07.2": "radius of one follicle = 22 ÷ 2 = 11 mm\nvolume of one follicle = (4 ÷ 3) × π × r<sup>3</sup>\nvolume = (4 ÷ 3) × 3.14 × 11<sup>3</sup>\nvolume of one follicle = 5572.5 mm<sup>3</sup>\nnumber of follicles = total volume ÷ volume of one follicle\nnumber = 39 000 ÷ 5572.5 = 6.99...\nnumber of follicles = <b>7</b>",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-02|02.5": "Destroying peat bogs and using peat <b>increases the temperature</b> of the atmosphere. When the peat is <b>burned as fuel</b> or is <b>decayed by microorganisms</b> in compost, the carbon it stores is released as <b>carbon dioxide</b>. Disturbing the bog also releases the <b>methane</b> trapped in it. Both gases <b>trap heat</b> escaping from the Earth, causing <b>global warming</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-03|03.3": "A full-mark graph has a <b>linear scale</b> on the vertical axis that fills at least half the grid, with the horizontal axis <b>labelled weeks</b>. All six points are <b>plotted correctly</b> to within half a small square, and a smooth <b>curved line of best fit</b> is drawn through them rather than joining point to point.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-05|05.6": "Hormonal contraceptives contain <b>oestrogen and progesterone</b>, or progesterone only. These hormones <b>inhibit FSH</b>, so no follicle or egg matures in the ovary, and they also <b>inhibit LH</b>, so <b>ovulation</b> does not happen. With no egg released there is nothing for sperm to fertilise. They can be given as the oral pill, an injection, an implant or a skin patch. To treat infertility, <b>FSH</b> is given to stimulate several eggs to mature and <b>LH</b> is given to trigger ovulation, so more eggs are available. In <b>IVF</b> the eggs are collected, fertilised outside the body and the embryos are placed in the uterus, and <b>progesterone</b> maintains the uterus lining so implantation is more likely.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-06|06.3": "Person 6 has polydactyly and is <b>heterozygous</b>, so her gametes carry <b>D or d</b>. Person 7 is unaffected, so he is dd and all his gametes carry <b>d</b>. Combining them gives offspring of <b>Dd, Dd, dd and dd</b>. Only the two <b>Dd</b> offspring have polydactyly, which is half of them, so the probability is <b>0.5</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-06|06.4": "The cystic fibrosis allele is <b>recessive</b>, so a person only has the disorder if they inherit <b>two CF alleles</b>, one from each parent. The chance of inheriting one CF allele is <b>1 in 50</b>, so the chance of inheriting two is <b>1/50 × 1/50 = 1/2500</b>. Carriers with only one allele are unaffected.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-07|07.1": "Energy needed by the chickens = 1000 × 46 = 46 000 MJ\nUsable energy in 1 kg of seeds = 16 × 0.8 = 12.8 MJ\nMass of seeds needed = 46 000 ÷ 12.8 = 3593.75 kg\nSeeds from 1 hectare = 16.4 × 1000 = 16 400 kg\nArea = 3593.75 ÷ 16 400 = 0.219131 hectares\nArea = 0.219131 × 10 000 = 2191.3 m<sup>2</sup>\nArea = <b>2190 m<sup>2</sup></b> to 3 significant figures",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-08|08.2": "In the kidney, small molecules including <b>glucose and urea are filtered</b> out of the blood, but <b>protein is not filtered</b> because its molecules are too large to pass through. As the filtrate flows along the tubule, <b>all of the glucose is reabsorbed</b> back into the blood, while <b>urea is not reabsorbed</b> and passes out of the body in the urine.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-08|08.3": "When the blood is too concentrated, more <b>ADH</b> is released. ADH increases the <b>permeability to water</b> of the <b>kidney tubules</b>, so more <b>water is reabsorbed</b> back into the blood. This means a <b>smaller volume</b> of urine is produced and it is <b>more concentrated</b>. When ADH levels fall, less water is reabsorbed and a large volume of dilute urine is made.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun24-09|09.1": "Spraying <b>glyphosate</b> kills the weeds in the field but does not harm the GM soya plants because they are <b>resistant</b> to it. This removes <b>competition</b> for light, water and mineral ions, so each soya plant receives more of these resources. With more light and water the plants carry out more <b>photosynthesis</b> and make more <b>glucose</b>, which is used in <b>respiration</b> to release energy, converted into cellulose for cell walls and stored as starch and oils in the beans. More <b>nitrate</b> is absorbed for making amino acids and <b>proteins</b>, and more <b>magnesium</b> is absorbed for making chlorophyll, so the plants grow larger and the yield of beans increases.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-03|03.2": "I would take pots of seedlings of the <b>same species, age and number</b> and give each the same volume of water, soil and <b>temperature</b>. One pot goes in a box with a <b>slit</b> so light comes from one direction only, and a second pot is left in <b>all-round light</b> as a control. I would <b>measure the initial height</b> of each shoot with a ruler, leave both for the <b>same length of time</b>, then measure the final height, straightening any bent shoots with a piece of thread, and record the direction in which the shoots have grown. I would then calculate the <b>mean increase in height</b> for each condition and <b>compare</b> the two sets of results.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-04|04.3": "Scientists use an <b>enzyme</b> to cut the <b>hGH gene</b> out of a human chromosome, and the same type of enzyme is used to cut open a <b>plasmid</b> taken from a bacterium. The gene is then <b>joined into the plasmid</b>, and the plasmid carrying the gene is <b>put into a bacterial cell</b>, which then makes human growth hormone.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-05|05.3": "Destroying peat bogs <b>reduces biodiversity</b> both locally and globally. Locally the specialised <b>habitat is lost</b>, so plants and animals no longer have food or shelter; some populations <b>migrate</b> away to find them, while species that can only live in bogs <b>die out or become extinct</b>. Globally, removing, burning and <b>decay</b> of the peat by microorganisms releases the <b>carbon dioxide</b> and <b>methane</b> that were trapped in it. These are greenhouse gases, so they increase <b>global warming</b> and cause <b>climate change</b>, including melting ice caps, rising sea levels, flooding and drought. This destroys habitats worldwide, changes migration patterns and drives more species to extinction.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-07|07.4": "<b>Receptors</b> in student B's eyes detect the <b>stimulus</b> of the rule starting to fall. This generates electrical <b>impulses</b> that travel along a <b>sensory neurone</b> in the <b>optic nerve</b> to the <b>brain</b>. At each <b>synapse</b> between neurones a <b>chemical</b> is released that diffuses across the gap and starts an impulse in the next neurone. The impulse then passes along a <b>motor neurone</b> to the <b>effector</b>, which is a muscle in the hand and arm. The muscle <b>contracts</b>, closing the fingers so the rule is caught.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-08|08.2": "Both parents are <b>heterozygous Hh</b>, so each produces gametes carrying <b>H or h</b>. Putting these into a Punnett square gives offspring of <b>HH, Hh, Hh and hh</b>. The <b>HH and Hh</b> offspring are Himalayan because H is dominant, and only <b>hh</b> is white. One box in four is hh, so the probability of a white offspring is <b>0.25</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-08|08.3": "Changing one base gives a <b>different triplet of three bases</b> in the gene, so a <b>different amino acid</b> is added when the protein is built. This changes the way the tyrosinase molecule folds, which <b>alters the shape of the active site</b>. The <b>tyrosine</b> substrate is then no longer <b>complementary</b> to the active site, so it cannot bind and no melanin is made.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-jun25-09|09.3": "Both investigations used a <b>large sample</b> of patients and ran for a long enough time for Hb-G to change, which makes the results more <b>reliable</b>, and comparing the drug group with a <b>control group</b> given a <b>placebo</b> means the fall in Hb-G can be credited to the drug. However, the trials would only be a <b>fair comparison</b> if the patients were matched for age, weight and diet, and the two drugs were not tested on the same people, so differences between the groups could affect the conclusion. Drug A is the more useful because it lowers the mean Hb-G value <b>below 7%</b>, which doctors accept, whereas drug B leaves patients above this figure, although neither returns patients to the normal range of 4.5–6%. Any <b>side effects</b> would also need to be known before deciding.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov20-04|04.2": "A nucleotide is made of three parts joined together. There is a <b>phosphate</b> group attached to a <b>sugar</b>, which is <b>deoxyribose</b>, and attached to the sugar there is one <b>base</b>. The base is one of <b>four</b> possible bases, <b>A, C, G or T</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov20-04|04.3": "Bases pair up, so number of pairs of nucleotides = 1.2 × 10<sup>10</sup> ÷ 2 = 6 × 10<sup>9</sup>\nLength = 6 × 10<sup>9</sup> × 0.34 = 2.04 × 10<sup>9</sup> nm\n1 nm = 1 × 10<sup>−9</sup> m\nLength = 2.04 × 10<sup>9</sup> × 1 × 10<sup>−9</sup>\nLength = <b>2.04 m</b>",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov20-05|05.4": "Person 7 has Dupuytren's and is <b>heterozygous Dd</b>, so his gametes carry <b>D or d</b>. Person 8 is unaffected, so she is <b>dd</b> and all her gametes carry <b>d</b>. The Punnett square gives offspring of <b>Dd, Dd, dd and dd</b>. The two <b>Dd</b> offspring have Dupuytren's because D is dominant, so two boxes out of four are affected and the probability is <b>0.5</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov20-07|07.3": "Insects need much <b>less land</b> to produce the same mass of food, so more land is freed up to <b>grow crops</b> for people. Insects also release far <b>less methane</b> than cattle and sheep, so there is less <b>global warming</b> and fewer harmful effects such as flooding and drought on <b>food production</b> in the future.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-02|02.3": "A full-mark graph has both axes <b>labelled</b> with day on the horizontal axis and pH on the vertical axis, using a <b>linear scale</b> of at least 1 cm per day that fills the grid. All the points from Table 1 are <b>plotted correctly</b> to within half a small square, and a smooth <b>curved line of best fit</b> is drawn through them.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-05|05.3": "Each quadrat has an area of 0.25 m<sup>2</sup>, so daisies per m<sup>2</sup> = 6 × 4 = 24\nArea of the triangle = ½ × 16 × 10 = 80 m<sup>2</sup>\nArea of the flower bed = 6 × 3 = 18 m<sup>2</sup>\nArea of lawn = 80 − 18 = 62 m<sup>2</sup>\nNumber of daisies = 24 × 62 = 1488\nNumber of daisies = <b>1490</b> to 3 significant figures",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-06|06.2": "<b>Receptors</b> in the skin of the hand detect the <b>stimulus</b> of the high temperature. This generates electrical <b>impulses</b> that pass along a <b>sensory neurone</b> to a <b>relay neurone</b> in the <b>spinal cord</b>, and then along a <b>motor neurone</b>. At each <b>synapse</b> between the neurones a <b>chemical</b> is released which diffuses across the gap and starts an impulse in the next neurone. The impulse reaches the <b>effector</b>, a muscle in the arm, which <b>contracts</b> to pull the hand away. The response is rapid because it is automatic and does not involve conscious thought.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-07|07.6": "Decrease in biomass = 2.3 − 0.1 = 2.2 million tonnes\nPercentage decrease = (2.2 ÷ 2.3) × 100\nPercentage decrease = 95.652...\nPercentage decrease = <b>96%</b>",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-08|08.3": "Person 8 has normal haemoglobin, so both gametes carry <b>H<sup>A</sup></b>. Person 9 has sickle cell trait and is heterozygous, so the gametes carry <b>H<sup>A</sup> or H<sup>S</sup></b>. The Punnett square gives offspring of <b>H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>A</sup>, H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>A</sup>, H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>S</sup> and H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>S</sup></b>. The H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>A</sup> children have normal haemoglobin and the <b>H<sup>A</sup>H<sup>S</sup></b> children have <b>sickle cell trait</b>, so the chance of sickle cell trait is 0.5. The chance of a girl is also 0.5, so the probability is 0.5 × 0.5 = <b>0.25</b>.",
  "aqa-8461-2h-nov21-09|09.5": "The finches that first colonised each island showed <b>variation</b> in beak depth, caused by different combinations of <b>alleles</b> of several genes and originally by <b>mutation</b>. Isabela is a much larger island with a wider <b>range of seed sizes</b>, including large, hard seeds, and the small ground finch also lives there, so there is <b>competition</b> for the smaller seeds. Birds with <b>larger, deeper beaks</b> can crack the bigger seeds, so they get enough food to <b>survive and reproduce</b> and <b>pass on the alleles</b> for deeper beaks to their offspring. On Daphne there is a narrower range of seeds and less competition, so a narrower range of beak depths is selected for. Over many generations this <b>natural selection</b> produces the different ranges.",
  "ks3-bio-cells-018": "The <b>permanent vacuole</b> is filled with <b>cell sap</b>, which is mostly water. When it is full it pushes the cytoplasm outwards against the cell wall, so the cell becomes firm, or <b>turgid</b>. The <b>cell wall</b> is made of <b>cellulose</b> and is strong, so it resists this outward push. The cell therefore keeps its shape and the cells together <b>support</b> the plant and hold it upright.",
  "ks3-bio-cells-021": "The tiny folds greatly increase the <b>surface area</b> of the cell membrane, so more digested food molecules can be absorbed each second. Small molecules such as <b>glucose</b> move into the cell by <b>diffusion</b>, from a high concentration in the gut to a lower concentration inside the cell. The many <b>mitochondria</b> are where <b>respiration</b> happens, releasing <b>energy</b>, and the cell uses this energy to take in food molecules when diffusion on its own is not fast enough.",
  "ks3-bio-eco-018": "The <b>DDT</b> is washed off the crops into rivers and taken in by small organisms such as algae. It is <b>not broken down or excreted</b>, so it stays in their bodies. Each predator eats many prey, so the <b>concentration increases at each step of the food chain</b>. Birds of prey are at the <b>top of the food chain</b>, so the DDT builds up to a level high enough to harm or kill them.",
  "ks3-bio-eco-021": "Trees are <b>producers</b>, so cutting them down removes the food at the start of many <b>food chains</b>. Animals lose their <b>habitat</b>, including their shelter and nesting sites. The animals that survive are crowded into a smaller area, so <b>competition</b> for food, water and space increases. Some organisms die and others <b>migrate</b> away, so <b>population sizes fall</b> and <b>biodiversity</b>, the number of different species living there, decreases. Because the species in a <b>food web</b> are all linked, losing one species also affects the animals that feed on it, so the whole ecosystem becomes <b>less stable</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-gas-018": "During an attack the <b>muscles around the bronchioles contract</b>, so the airways become <b>narrower</b>. The lining also becomes <b>inflamed</b> and makes extra <b>mucus</b>, narrowing them further. Less air reaches the <b>alveoli</b> with each breath, so less <b>oxygen</b> is absorbed and the person is breathless and wheezy. A reliever <b>inhaler</b> contains a drug that <b>relaxes the muscles</b> around the bronchioles, so the airways widen again.",
  "ks3-bio-gas-019": "The <b>mucus</b> is sticky and <b>traps dust and bacteria</b> in the air being breathed in. The <b>cilia</b> beat and <b>sweep the mucus up</b> away from the lungs to the throat, where it is swallowed. Smoke <b>paralyses and destroys the cilia</b>, so the mucus is no longer swept away. Mucus and the trapped bacteria then collect in the lungs, so the smoker <b>coughs</b> and is more likely to get <b>chest infections</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-gas-022": "Destroying the walls between the alveoli greatly reduces the total <b>surface area</b> of the lungs. A smaller surface area means less <b>oxygen</b> can <b>diffuse</b> into the blood each second, so the blood leaving the lungs carries <b>less oxygen</b> than normal. Climbing stairs needs more energy, so the leg muscles must <b>respire faster</b> and <b>demand more oxygen</b>. The damaged lungs cannot supply this extra oxygen, so the person breathes faster and more deeply and still feels very <b>breathless</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-health-018": "Random <b>mutations</b> happen in bacteria, and by chance one may make a bacterium <b>resistant</b> to the antibiotic. When the antibiotic is used the non-resistant bacteria are <b>killed</b> but the resistant ones <b>survive</b>. The survivors <b>reproduce</b> and pass on the resistance, so the resistant population grows. Doctors slow this down by only <b>prescribing antibiotics when they are really needed</b> and telling patients to finish the whole course.",
  "ks3-bio-health-019": "Give the new painkiller to one large group of patients and the existing one to a second, similar group. <b>Match the groups</b> for age, sex and how severe their pain is, so the comparison is <b>fair</b>. Do not tell the patients which drug they are taking, and ideally do not tell the doctors either, so the trial is <b>double blind</b> and there is no <b>bias</b>. Then measure the pain relief in each group and compare the <b>mean</b> results.",
  "ks3-bio-health-021": "The <b>tar</b> in smoke contains <b>carcinogens</b>, which increase the risk of <b>lung cancer</b>. Smoke <b>paralyses the cilia</b> lining the airways, so mucus builds up and causes smoker's cough and chest infections, and it damages the walls of the <b>alveoli</b>, reducing the <b>surface area</b> for gas exchange and causing <b>emphysema</b>. <b>Carbon monoxide</b> binds to <b>haemoglobin</b> in the red blood cells, so the blood carries <b>less oxygen</b> to the tissues. <b>Nicotine</b> raises heart rate and <b>blood pressure</b> and narrows blood vessels, increasing the risk of <b>heart attack and stroke</b>. If a smoker gives up, no new damage is done, the cilia recover and the blood carries more oxygen, so the risk falls over time.",
  "ks3-bio-inh-018": "There is <b>variation</b> in the population, so by chance some rabbits already have thicker fur. In the colder winters these rabbits <b>lose less heat</b> and are <b>more likely to survive</b>, so they are more likely to <b>reproduce</b> and pass on the <b>genes</b> for thick fur. Over many generations the proportion of thick-furred rabbits increases and the population becomes better <b>adapted</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-inh-019": "A rapid change means individuals that were well <b>adapted</b> to the old conditions are no longer suited to the new ones. They <b>compete</b> less successfully for resources such as food and shelter, so fewer of them survive. If no individuals happen to have <b>characteristics</b> suited to the new conditions, none survive to <b>reproduce</b>. <b>Natural selection</b> needs many generations, so there is too little time to adapt and the species becomes <b>extinct</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-inh-022": "There was <b>variation</b> in the original population, so a small number of moths were dark by chance because of a <b>mutation</b>. Once the bark was blackened, pale moths stood out and were easily seen and eaten by <b>predators</b> such as birds. The dark moths were <b>camouflaged</b> against the black bark, so they were less likely to be eaten and were more likely to <b>survive</b> to breeding age and <b>reproduce</b>. They passed the <b>genes</b> for dark colour on to their offspring, so over many generations the proportion of dark moths increased until almost all were dark. This is <b>natural selection</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-nutr-017": "The small intestine is <b>very long</b>, which gives it a large <b>surface area</b> for absorption. Its lining is folded and covered in <b>villi</b>, which increase the surface area even more. The wall of each villus is only <b>one cell thick</b>, so there is a <b>short diffusion distance</b> into the blood. Each villus also has a <b>good blood supply</b>, which carries the absorbed molecules away and keeps a steep <b>concentration gradient</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-nutr-018": "In the <b>mouth</b> the bread is chewed and mixed with saliva, and the enzyme <b>amylase</b> starts to break the <b>starch</b> down into sugars. It is swallowed and pushed down the <b>oesophagus</b> by muscles in the wall. In the <b>stomach</b> it is churned with <b>hydrochloric acid</b> and protease. In the <b>small intestine</b> digestion is completed and the small soluble molecules are <b>absorbed</b> into the blood, then <b>water is absorbed</b> in the <b>large intestine</b> and the waste that is left is <b>egested</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-nutr-022": "This diet is <b>unbalanced</b> because it is almost all <b>carbohydrate</b>. The excess energy is stored as <b>fat</b>, so the teenager could become <b>obese</b>. The sugary drinks feed bacteria in the mouth that make acid, causing <b>tooth decay</b>. There is very little <b>protein</b>, which is needed for <b>growth and repair</b>, so growth could be poor. There is little <b>iron</b>, so too little <b>haemoglobin</b> is made and the teenager could develop <b>anaemia</b> and feel tired. There is little <b>calcium</b> or vitamin D, so bones and teeth may be weak, and very little <b>fibre</b>, so food moves slowly through the gut and <b>constipation</b> is likely.",
  "ks3-bio-photo-018": "<b>Photosynthesis</b> transfers <b>light energy</b> from the Sun into a chemical energy store in <b>glucose</b>. Plants and algae are the <b>producers</b> at the start of nearly every <b>food chain</b>, so all other organisms depend on them for food. Photosynthesis also releases the <b>oxygen</b> in the atmosphere, which almost all organisms need for <b>aerobic respiration</b>, and it removes <b>carbon dioxide</b>, helping to keep the atmosphere in balance.",
  "ks3-bio-photo-019": "Moving the lamp closer increases the <b>light intensity</b> reaching the pondweed. More light energy is absorbed by the <b>chlorophyll</b>, so the <b>rate of photosynthesis</b> increases and more <b>oxygen</b> bubbles are given off. Once the light is very bright, light is no longer the factor <b>limiting</b> the rate. Something else, such as the <b>carbon dioxide</b> concentration or the <b>temperature</b>, is now the limiting factor, so the rate stays the same.",
  "ks3-bio-photo-021": "The leaf is broad and flat, giving a large <b>surface area</b> to absorb <b>light</b>. The <b>palisade cells</b> near the top are packed with <b>chloroplasts</b> containing <b>chlorophyll</b> to absorb that light. The leaf is <b>thin</b>, so <b>carbon dioxide</b> only has a short distance to <b>diffuse</b> to the photosynthesising cells. <b>Stomata</b> in the lower surface let carbon dioxide in and oxygen out, and <b>air spaces</b> inside let gases move around freely. Veins containing <b>xylem</b> bring water to the cells and <b>phloem</b> carries the sugars away.",
  "ks3-bio-repro-019": "The <b>anthers</b> hang outside the flower so the pollen is easily blown away by the wind. The <b>stigmas</b> are large and <b>feathery</b> so they catch pollen grains from the air. The pollen grains themselves are <b>small, light and smooth</b> so they can be carried a long way, and the flower makes very <b>large amounts of pollen</b> because most of it never lands on a stigma.",
  "ks3-bio-repro-020": "The <b>placenta</b> is an organ attached to the lining of the <b>uterus</b> and joined to the fetus by the <b>umbilical cord</b>. It has a very large <b>surface area</b> and brings the mother's blood and the fetus's blood very close together without letting them <b>mix</b>. <b>Oxygen</b> and nutrients such as glucose <b>diffuse</b> from the mother's blood into the fetus, and waste products such as <b>carbon dioxide and urea</b> diffuse the other way into the mother's blood.",
  "ks3-bio-repro-021": "Both are <b>sexual reproduction</b>, so both need a male and a female <b>gamete</b> to join. In humans the gametes are the <b>sperm and egg</b>, while in a plant they are the nucleus in the <b>pollen grain</b> and the nucleus in the <b>ovule</b>. Human gametes are brought together by mating and <b>fertilisation is internal</b>, in the oviduct, whereas plant gametes are brought together by <b>pollination</b> by wind or animals and the male nucleus must travel down a <b>pollen tube</b> to the ovule. A human embryo develops in the <b>uterus</b> protected by <b>amniotic fluid</b>, while a plant embryo develops inside a <b>seed</b> protected by a seed coat and supplied with a food store.",
  "ks3-bio-resp-018": "The student should seal germinating seeds in a flask connected to a test tube of <b>limewater</b> and draw air through the apparatus. If the limewater <b>turns cloudy</b> this shows <b>carbon dioxide</b> is being produced. As a <b>control</b>, an identical flask of seeds that have been <b>boiled and cooled</b> should be set up. Its limewater should stay clear, proving the gas came from living, <b>respiring</b> seeds.",
  "ks3-bio-resp-021": "<b>Aerobic respiration</b> uses <b>oxygen</b> while <b>anaerobic respiration</b> does not. The word equation for aerobic respiration is <b>glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water</b>, whereas in muscle the anaerobic equation is <b>glucose → lactic acid</b>. Aerobic respiration breaks glucose down completely so it <b>releases much more energy</b> from each glucose molecule. At rest and during gentle exercise the blood delivers enough oxygen, so muscles respire aerobically. During very hard exercise oxygen cannot be delivered fast enough, so muscles also respire anaerobically, giving extra energy but building up <b>lactic acid</b>, which causes <b>fatigue</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-skel-001": "The skeleton gives <b>support</b>, holding the body up and giving it its shape. It gives <b>protection</b> to delicate organs, for example the skull protects the brain and the ribs protect the heart and lungs. It allows <b>movement</b> because muscles are attached to the bones. Finally, the <b>bone marrow</b> inside some bones makes <b>blood cells</b>.",
  "ks3-bio-skel-018": "The <b>bones</b> of the upper and lower leg are rigid and act as <b>levers</b> that can be moved. The knee <b>joint</b> lets these two bones move relative to each other. A <b>muscle</b> in the thigh <b>contracts</b> and gets shorter, producing a <b>pulling force</b>. The <b>tendons</b> are <b>inelastic</b>, so they do not stretch and instead transmit this force from the muscle to the bone, which moves.",
  "ks3-bio-skel-020": "Use a <b>newton meter</b> or hand grip meter and record the <b>force</b> for every squeeze. Keep the same person using the <b>same hand</b> and squeezing as <b>hard as possible</b> each time, so only the number of squeezes changes. Do a set number of squeezes, for example 20, with the <b>same short rest</b> between each one. Then <b>repeat</b> the whole experiment and work out a <b>mean</b> force for each squeeze number.",
  "ks3-bio-skel-021": "The bones of the leg are rigid and act as <b>levers</b>. The hip and knee <b>joints</b> let the bones move, and <b>cartilage</b> and <b>synovial fluid</b> reduce <b>friction</b> between them. Muscles in the thigh <b>contract</b> and get shorter, producing a <b>pulling force</b>, and the <b>tendons</b> transmit this force to the bone so the lower leg swings forwards to strike the ball. The muscles work in <b>antagonistic pairs</b>, so while one muscle contracts the opposite one <b>relaxes</b>, and the pair swap over to bring the leg back again.",
  "ks3-chem-acids-019": "Warm the dilute sulfuric acid and add the copper oxide a little at a time, stirring, until <b>no more reacts</b> and some solid is left over, showing the acid has all been used up. Then <b>filter</b> the mixture to remove the <b>unreacted copper oxide</b>. Heat the <b>filtrate</b> in an evaporating basin to evaporate some of the water and leave a <b>saturated solution</b>. Finally leave it to cool and <b>crystallise</b>, then pat the crystals dry between filter papers.",
  "ks3-chem-acids-021": "Add a few drops of <b>universal indicator</b> to a sample of each solution. The one that turns the indicator <b>red</b> is the hydrochloric acid, because it is strongly acidic. The one that turns the indicator <b>purple</b> is the sodium hydroxide, because it is strongly alkaline. The one that stays <b>green</b> is the pure water, because it is <b>neutral with a pH of 7</b>. To confirm the acid, add a small piece of <b>magnesium ribbon</b>: bubbles of <b>hydrogen</b> are given off, which burn with a <b>squeaky pop</b>.",
  "ks3-chem-acids-023": "The samples at <b>pH 4 and pH 6</b> are <b>acidic</b> and need treating, with the pH 4 bed needing the most treatment because it is the most acidic. She should add a <b>base</b> such as <b>calcium hydroxide (lime)</b> or calcium carbonate to these beds. The base <b>neutralises</b> the acid, raising the pH towards 7. The sample at <b>pH 9</b> is <b>alkaline</b>, so adding an acidic material such as composted bark or sulfur would lower its pH. Adding water only <b>dilutes</b> the soil solution and drains away, so it does not remove the acid permanently.",
  "ks3-chem-atoms-021": "The copper is an <b>element</b> because it contains only copper atoms. The copper oxide is a <b>compound</b> because copper and oxygen atoms are <b>chemically joined</b> in a fixed ratio. The iron filings and sand are a <b>mixture</b> because the two substances are <b>not chemically joined</b>. The mixture can be separated <b>physically</b>, for example using a magnet, while the copper oxide would need a <b>chemical reaction</b> to split it up.",
  "ks3-chem-atoms-022": "The conclusion is wrong because mass is always <b>conserved</b>: atoms are only <b>rearranged</b> in a chemical reaction and are never created or destroyed. When wood burns, gases such as <b>carbon dioxide</b> and <b>water vapour</b> are made and these <b>escape into the air</b>, so they are not included in the mass on the balance. <b>Oxygen</b> from the air also joins the reaction but was not weighed at the start. If the reaction were carried out in a <b>sealed container</b> on the balance, all the reactants and products would be trapped and the total mass would stay the same.",
  "ks3-chem-earth-006": "Existing rocks are first broken down by <b>weathering</b> and <b>erosion</b> into small fragments. These fragments are <b>transported</b> by rivers and then <b>deposited</b> as layers of <b>sediment</b>, often on the sea bed. The weight of the layers above squeezes out the water and <b>compacts</b> the sediment. Finally, <b>dissolved minerals cement</b> the grains together to make solid rock.",
  "ks3-chem-earth-022": "Recycling aluminium uses <b>far less energy</b> than extracting it from its ore by <b>electrolysis</b>. That means less <b>fossil fuel</b> is burned, so less <b>carbon dioxide</b> is released into the atmosphere. Aluminium ore is a <b>finite resource</b>, so recycling makes the reserves last longer and reduces the damage caused by <b>quarrying</b>. Less waste also goes to <b>landfill</b>, saving space and reducing pollution. Against this, the scheme <b>costs money</b> to set up and run, and the lorries that collect the cans burn fuel and cause pollution of their own.",
  "ks3-chem-earth-023": "The granite is first broken down by <b>weathering</b>, for example by water freezing in cracks and expanding to split the rock. The loose fragments are then <b>eroded and transported</b> away by rivers, wind or ice. When the water slows down the fragments are <b>deposited</b> as layers of <b>sediment</b>, often on a sea bed. More layers build up on top and <b>compact</b> the sediment, squeezing out the water. <b>Dissolved minerals cement</b> the grains together to form a <b>sedimentary rock</b>. If this rock is buried very deeply it is subjected to <b>high temperature and pressure</b> without melting, so its minerals <b>recrystallise</b> and it becomes a <b>metamorphic rock</b>.",
  "ks3-chem-earth-024": "Fossil fuels contain <b>carbon</b>, so burning them releases <b>carbon dioxide</b> into the atmosphere. Carbon dioxide is a <b>greenhouse gas</b>, which absorbs the <b>infrared radiation</b> given off by the Earth's surface. It re-radiates some of this energy back towards the surface, so <b>less energy escapes into space</b> and the average global temperature rises. One action a country could take is to generate electricity from <b>renewable sources</b> such as wind, solar or hydroelectricity instead of coal and gas. Another is to <b>insulate buildings</b> better, switch transport to electricity or <b>plant more trees</b> to absorb carbon dioxide.",
  "ks3-chem-energetics-020": "Burning the candle is <b>exothermic</b>, because energy is transferred <b>from the chemical store</b> of the wax <b>to the surroundings</b>, so the temperature of the surrounding air <b>rises</b>. Melting ice is <b>endothermic</b>, because energy is transferred <b>from the surroundings into the ice</b>, so the temperature of the surroundings <b>falls</b> and the ice itself stays at <b>0 °C</b> until it has all melted.",
  "ks3-chem-energetics-021": "Measure a fixed mass of water into a <b>copper calorimeter</b> and record its starting temperature. Weigh a <b>spirit burner</b> containing fuel A, burn it under the water while stirring until the temperature has risen by a set amount, then reweigh the burner to find the <b>mass of fuel burned</b>. Repeat with fuel B using the <b>same mass of water</b>, the same temperature rise and the same distance from flame to beaker. Work out the energy using <b>energy = mass of water × 4.2 × temperature rise</b> and divide by the mass of fuel burned. The fuel giving the greater <b>energy per gram</b> is the better choice, though cost, safety and availability also matter.",
  "ks3-chem-energetics-022": "Temperature rise = 35 − 18 = <b>17 °C</b>\nEnergy = mass × 4.2 × temperature rise\nEnergy = 200 × 4.2 × 17\nEnergy = <b>14 280 J</b>\nEnergy per gram = 14 280 ÷ 0.50\nEnergy per gram = <b>28 560 J/g</b>, which is about <b>28.6 kJ/g</b>",
  "ks3-chem-materials-020": "Glass is <b>heavy and brittle</b> so it can shatter, although it can be <b>recycled repeatedly</b> without losing quality. Aluminium is <b>light</b> and does not shatter and is <b>easy to recycle</b>, but it is <b>opaque</b> so you cannot see the drink inside. The polymer is the <b>lightest</b> and will not break, but it is made from <b>crude oil</b> and is harder to recycle repeatedly. For a sports event the <b>polymer</b> is most suitable because it is light to carry and will not shatter dangerously if dropped in a crowd.",
  "ks3-chem-materials-022": "Wood is <b>cheap</b> and comes from a <b>renewable</b> source, but it <b>rots</b> and needs regular painting to protect it from the weather. uPVC does not rot or corrode and is a good <b>thermal insulator</b>, but it is made from <b>crude oil</b>, a finite resource, and is difficult to recycle. Aluminium is strong, light and <b>resists corrosion</b>, so the frames can be thin, but it is a good <b>conductor</b> of heat so more energy escapes through the frame. Extracting aluminium also needs a huge amount of <b>electricity</b>, making it expensive and high in carbon emissions. I would recommend <b>uPVC</b>, because it needs almost no maintenance and its good insulation would lower the school's heating bills.",
  "ks3-chem-materials-023": "Most metals are <b>reactive</b>, so over millions of years they have reacted with oxygen or other elements and are now found as <b>compounds</b> in <b>ores</b>. Only very <b>unreactive</b> metals such as gold and platinum are found as the pure element. Metals <b>less reactive than carbon</b>, such as iron, copper and zinc, can be extracted by heating their oxides with <b>carbon</b>, because the more reactive carbon <b>removes the oxygen</b> from the metal oxide. Metals <b>more reactive than carbon</b>, such as aluminium and sodium, will not be displaced by carbon and so must be extracted by <b>electrolysis</b>, which uses a great deal of electricity and is therefore expensive.",
  "ks3-chem-particles-018": "In a liquid the particles are <b>close together and touching</b>, while in a gas they are <b>far apart</b>. In a liquid the particles are <b>randomly arranged</b>, and in a gas they are random but spread right out. Liquid particles <b>slide over one another</b>, whereas gas particles <b>move quickly in all directions</b>. The <b>forces of attraction</b> between the particles are much weaker in a gas than in a liquid.",
  "ks3-chem-particles-021": "Energy is <b>transferred from the warm room</b> to the dry ice. Its particles gain energy and <b>vibrate faster</b> until the forces holding them in fixed positions are overcome. The particles then escape straight into the gas state without ever forming a liquid, which is called <b>sublimation</b>. In the gas the particles are far apart and move randomly, so they spread out through the room by <b>diffusion</b>. No particles are <b>created or destroyed</b>, so the carbon dioxide particles are now part of the air and the mass of the air has increased by the mass of the dry ice.",
  "ks3-chem-particles-022": "In a gas the particles are <b>very far apart</b> with large <b>spaces</b> between them, so pushing the plunger simply forces them <b>closer together</b> and the volume decreases from 50 cm<sup>3</sup> to 30 cm<sup>3</sup>. In a liquid the particles are already <b>touching</b>, with almost no space between them, so the water <b>cannot be compressed</b> into a smaller volume and the plunger will not move. As the air is squashed, the <b>same number of particles</b> occupies a smaller volume, so they <b>hit the walls more often</b> and the <b>pressure increases</b>.",
  "ks3-chem-periodic-021": "Element X belongs to <b>Group 1</b>, the <b>alkali metals</b>. Group 1 metals are <b>soft</b> enough to be cut with a knife, which matches the description. They react <b>vigorously with cold water</b> to release <b>hydrogen</b> gas, which is the gas that burns with a squeaky pop. The other product is a <b>metal hydroxide</b> solution, which is strongly <b>alkaline</b> and so gives the high pH of 13.",
  "ks3-chem-periodic-023": "The <b>alkali metals</b> are <b>soft solids</b> at room temperature, whereas the <b>noble gases</b> are all <b>gases</b>. The alkali metals are <b>extremely reactive</b>, reacting vigorously with water and oxygen, while the noble gases are almost completely <b>unreactive</b> and normally take part in no chemical reactions at all. Reactivity <b>increases going down Group 1</b>, so caesium is much more reactive than lithium, but the noble gases stay unreactive all the way down Group 0, so there is no similar increase. Lithium is used in <b>rechargeable batteries</b> because it reacts readily, whereas <b>argon</b> fills light bulbs precisely because it will not react with the hot filament.",
  "ks3-chem-reactions-020": "As the temperature increases the magnesium <b>disappears in a shorter time</b>, so the <b>rate of reaction increases</b>. This is because the particles gain energy and <b>move faster</b> at higher temperatures. They therefore <b>collide more frequently</b> with the surface of the magnesium, and a greater proportion of those collisions have <b>enough energy to react</b>.",
  "ks3-chem-reactions-022": "The <b>magnesium</b> reacts very <b>vigorously</b>, bubbling rapidly and disappearing quickly. The <b>zinc</b> reacts <b>steadily</b>, with a moderate rate of bubbling. The <b>iron</b> reacts only <b>slowly</b>, giving a few bubbles. The <b>copper does not react at all</b> because it is less reactive than hydrogen and so cannot displace it from the acid. The more vigorously a metal reacts with acid the more reactive it is, so the <b>order of reactivity</b> is magnesium, zinc, iron, then copper from most to least reactive.",
  "ks3-chem-reactions-023": "Heating the green copper carbonate is <b>thermal decomposition</b>, because one compound breaks down into two on heating. The word equation is <b>copper carbonate → copper oxide + carbon dioxide</b>. The black solid is <b>copper oxide</b> and the gas is <b>carbon dioxide</b>, which is why the limewater turned cloudy. Heating the copper oxide with carbon is a <b>displacement</b>, or <b>reduction</b>, reaction because the more reactive carbon takes the oxygen away. The word equation is <b>copper oxide + carbon → copper + carbon dioxide</b>, and the orange-brown solid is copper.",
  "ks3-chem-reactions-024": "Mean rate over the first 20 s = 18 ÷ 20\nMean rate = <b>0.9 cm<sup>3</sup>/s</b>\nMean rate over the whole reaction = 36 ÷ 60\nMean rate = <b>0.6 cm<sup>3</sup>/s</b>\nThe rate falls because the magnesium is gradually <b>used up</b>, so there is less <b>surface area</b> in contact with the acid. Fewer <b>collisions</b> happen each second, so the rate drops and becomes zero once all the magnesium has reacted.",
  "ks3-chem-separating-019": "Measure <b>100 cm<sup>3</sup> of water</b> into a beaker and heat it to a chosen temperature. Add copper sulfate a little at a time, <b>stirring</b>, until no more will dissolve and the solution is <b>saturated</b>. Record the <b>total mass</b> of copper sulfate added, then repeat at several different temperatures. The <b>volume of water</b> must be kept the same every time so the comparison is fair.",
  "ks3-chem-separating-021": "She should draw a <b>pencil</b> line near the bottom of the chromatography paper and put a small, equally spaced spot of the food colouring and of each known dye on the line. The paper is then stood in a shallow layer of <b>solvent</b>, making sure the solvent level is <b>below the pencil line</b>. The solvent rises up the paper and the paper is removed before it reaches the top, marking the <b>solvent front</b>. If a spot from the food colouring has travelled the <b>same distance</b> as a spot from a known dye, that dye is present. A spot that <b>matches none</b> of the legal dyes shows another substance is present, supporting her idea.",
  "ks3-chem-separating-022": "First <b>filter</b> the mixture using filter paper in a funnel, so the insoluble grit is trapped as the <b>residue</b>. Collect the blue <b>filtrate</b>, which is the copper sulfate solution, in a clean beaker. Heat the filtrate gently in an <b>evaporating basin</b> over a <b>water bath</b> to evaporate some of the water. Stop heating once crystals start to form at the edge and leave the solution to cool and <b>crystallise</b> slowly, then pat the crystals dry. Using a water bath rather than a direct flame avoids <b>spitting</b> of hot solution, and <b>eye protection</b> should be worn.",
  "ks3-phys-elec-019": "As the fuel rubs against the pipe, <b>charge builds up by friction</b> on the tanker. If enough <b>static charge</b> collects, a <b>spark</b> can jump between the tanker and the ground. A spark could <b>ignite the fuel vapour</b> and cause an explosion. Connecting the metal wire <b>earths</b> the tanker, so the charge flows <b>safely away to the ground</b> instead of building up.",
  "ks3-phys-elec-021": "In a <b>series</b> circuit there is only <b>one path</b>, so the <b>same current</b> flows through every component. The <b>potential difference</b> of the supply is <b>shared</b> between the components, so a set of lamps in series would all be <b>dim</b>. Also, if one lamp breaks the circuit is broken and <b>all the lamps go out</b>. In a <b>parallel</b> circuit each lamp is on its own <b>branch</b> and receives the <b>full supply potential difference</b>, so every lamp is at full brightness. If one lamp breaks, the <b>others keep working</b>, and each can be <b>switched on and off independently</b>, which is why houses are wired this way.",
  "ks3-phys-energy-008": "The air next to the radiator is heated, so its particles gain energy and <b>move further apart</b>. This makes the warm air <b>less dense</b>, so it <b>rises</b>. Cooler, <b>denser</b> air then <b>sinks</b> to take its place next to the radiator. This sets up a circulating <b>convection current</b> that carries energy all around the room.",
  "ks3-phys-energy-012": "They could fit <b>double glazing</b>, because the layer of <b>trapped air</b> between the two panes is a poor <b>conductor</b>, which reduces conduction through the windows. They could fit <b>loft insulation</b>, because the air trapped in the fibres reduces conduction and stops <b>convection currents</b> carrying energy out through the roof. They could also fit <b>draught excluders</b> under the doors, which stops warm air being carried out of the house and cold air being drawn in. Together these changes reduce the energy transferred to the surroundings, so the heating bills fall.",
  "ks3-phys-energy-015": "The wind farm does not release <b>carbon dioxide</b> or other polluting gases while it is generating, and the wind is <b>renewable</b> so there are <b>no fuel costs</b>. The coal-fired power station can generate electricity <b>at any time</b>, whatever the weather, so its supply is <b>reliable</b>, and it produces a large output from a fairly <b>small area of land</b>.",
  "ks3-phys-forces-017": "At first her <b>weight</b> is much greater than the <b>air resistance</b>, so there is a large <b>resultant force</b> downwards and she <b>accelerates</b>. As her speed increases the air resistance on her <b>increases</b>, until the air resistance <b>equals her weight</b>. The forces are then <b>balanced</b> and she falls at a steady <b>terminal speed</b>. Opening the parachute greatly increases the <b>surface area</b>, so air resistance rises sharply and becomes greater than her weight, giving a resultant force upwards so she <b>slows down</b>. As she slows, the air resistance falls until it again equals her weight, and she falls at a new, slower steady speed until she lands.",
  "ks3-phys-light-022": "Light from the part of the straw below the surface travels <b>out of the water into the air</b>. Light travels <b>more slowly in water than in air</b>, so its speed <b>increases</b> as it leaves the water. This change of speed makes the light <b>change direction</b>, or <b>refract</b>, at the surface, bending <b>away from the normal</b>. The brain always assumes that light has travelled in a <b>straight line</b> from the object to the eye, so the underwater part of the straw appears to be in a different place and the straw looks bent at the surface.",
  "ks3-phys-mag-014": "Pressing the switch <b>completes the circuit</b>, so a current flows and the <b>electromagnet</b> becomes magnetised. It <b>attracts the iron arm</b>, so the hammer strikes the gong and makes a sound. As the arm moves it <b>breaks the circuit</b> at a contact, so the current stops and the electromagnet <b>loses its magnetism</b>. The <b>springy arm</b> then returns and remakes the contact, so the cycle <b>repeats rapidly</b> to give a continuous ringing.",
  "ks3-phys-mag-015": "When the current flows in the coil it creates a <b>magnetic field</b> around the coil. This field <b>interacts with the field of the permanent magnet</b>. The forces produced on the two <b>opposite sides of the coil act in opposite directions</b>, which makes the coil <b>turn</b>. A <b>split-ring commutator</b> reverses the current every <b>half turn</b>, so the coil keeps rotating in the same direction instead of stopping.",
  "ks3-phys-mag-022": "Wind a coil of <b>insulated wire</b> around an <b>iron nail</b> and connect it to a power supply through a switch. Test the strength of the electromagnet by counting how many <b>steel paperclips</b> it can pick up. Repeat this with different <b>numbers of turns</b>, for example 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50. Keep the <b>current the same</b> each time, checking it with an <b>ammeter</b>, and also keep the same core, the same power supply setting and the same type of paperclip. <b>Repeat each measurement three times and take a mean</b>. The results should show that more turns pick up more paperclips, so more turns give a stronger electromagnet.",
  "ks3-phys-matter-022": "The air in the can is made of <b>particles that move randomly</b> in all directions. These particles <b>collide with the walls</b> of the can, and it is these collisions that produce the <b>pressure</b>. Heating the can in the sun transfers energy to the <b>kinetic store</b> of the particles, so on average they <b>move faster</b>. Because they are moving faster they <b>hit the walls more often</b>, and <b>each collision exerts a larger force</b>. The total force on each unit of area therefore rises, so the pressure increases until the can bursts.",
  "ks3-phys-sound-014": "The sound wave travels down the <b>ear canal</b> and makes the <b>eardrum vibrate</b>. These vibrations are passed on and made <b>larger</b> by the <b>three small bones</b> of the middle ear. The bones pass the vibrations to the <b>cochlea</b>, which is filled with liquid. The cochlea turns the vibrations into <b>electrical signals</b> that travel along the <b>auditory nerve</b> to the brain.",
  "ks3-phys-sound-019": "Total distance = speed × time\nTotal distance = 1500 × 4\nTotal distance = <b>6000 m</b>\nThe pulse travels <b>down to the seabed and back</b>, so the depth is <b>half</b> the total distance.\nDepth = 6000 ÷ 2\nDepth = <b>3000 m</b>",
  "ks3-phys-sound-022": "In a sound wave it is the <b>particles of a material</b> that vibrate, so sound needs a <b>medium</b> to travel through. Light is a wave of vibrating <b>electric and magnetic fields</b>, so it needs no particles at all. Sound is a <b>longitudinal</b> wave, with the particles vibrating <b>along</b> the direction the wave travels, whereas light is a <b>transverse</b> wave, with vibrations at <b>right angles</b> to the direction of travel. Sound travels at about <b>330 m/s</b> in air while light travels at about <b>300 000 000 m/s</b>. Sound cannot travel through a <b>vacuum</b> but light can, which is why we can see the Sun but never hear it.",
  "ks3-phys-space-003": "The Earth's <b>axis is tilted</b> as it orbits the Sun. In summer the <b>northern hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun</b>, so the Sun is <b>higher in the sky</b> and the sunlight strikes the ground more <b>directly</b>, concentrating the same energy on a <b>smaller area</b>. The <b>days are also longer</b>, so there are more hours of heating each day, making the UK warmer.",
  "ks3-phys-space-022": "The student is wrong because the Earth's <b>distance from the Sun barely changes</b> during the year, so distance cannot explain the seasons. The real reason is that the Earth's <b>axis is tilted</b> by about <b>23 degrees</b> and keeps pointing in the same direction as the Earth orbits. For part of the year the <b>northern hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun</b>, so sunlight hits the ground more <b>directly</b>, concentrating the energy on a smaller area, and <b>daylight lasts longer</b>. At the same time the <b>southern hemisphere is tilted away</b> from the Sun, so when it is summer in the United Kingdom it is <b>winter in Australia</b>."
});
